      <p class="eyebrow">Academic & Educational Resources</p>
      <h1>Latent Form Theory</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A philosophical framework exploring form, material, perception, and judgment across creative disciplines.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="content">

      <h2>One-Page Summary of Latent Form Theory</h2>

      <p>
        Latent Form Theory is an interdisciplinary framework developed to explore a recurring question across craft, design, art, and writing: does coherent form emerge primarily through accumulation, or through disciplined acts of selection and subtraction?
      </p>

      <p>
        Latent Form Theory (LFT) is a philosophy of making, organized as a three-volume work — <em>Method</em>, <em>Matter</em>, and <em>Maker</em> — that treats creation not as invention but as recognition.
      </p>


      <h2>Scope</h2>

      <p>
        Latent Form Theory is offered as a philosophical framework rather than an empirical scientific theory. Its purpose is to provide concepts and language for thinking about making, perception, material, and judgment across creative disciplines.
      </p>


      <h2>Status of the Work</h2>

      <p>
        Latent Form Theory is an independently developed philosophical framework. The website serves as the canonical reference for the current edition of the work. While the ideas continue to evolve through publication and discussion, each published volume is considered a stable reference suitable for citation.
      </p>


      <h2>The Three Volumes</h2>

      <h3>Volume I — <em>Latent Form Theory: The Philosophy of Essential Subtraction</em></h3>

      <p>
        Volume I argues that material is never a blank canvas. A board or a stone already carries a history of growth, pressure, and time, and the maker's task is to reveal the form already latent within it through disciplined removal rather than imposed addition.
      </p>

      <p>
        The volume traces this process from the first reduction — an act of clarification rather than domination — through the "axis of resolution," the point where material, geometry, and intention align, to completion understood not as the moment nothing more can be done, but the moment nothing more needs to be done.
      </p>


      <h3>Volume II — <em>Modern Stratigraphy: Material Memory and the Organization of Earth</em></h3>

      <p>
        Volume II shifts from how form emerges to what is already present before the maker arrives. It proposes that natural materials are not passive media but historical records: mineral pigments carry geological time, hardwood carries biological time, and finished objects become meeting points between natural history and human perception.
      </p>

      <p>
        From this follows a distinction central to the trilogy: composition as an element structurally integral to the piece, versus addition as an element merely applied onto it.
      </p>


      <h3>Volume III — <em>The Disciplined Eye: Perception, Judgment, and the Ethics of Seeing</em></h3>

      <p>
        Volume III turns from the object to the observer, arguing that the failure to perceive latent order is a failure of trained attention rather than a property of the world.
      </p>

      <p>
        Its central move is redefining taste: not preference, but the capacity to distinguish legible ornament from inertial ornament. The volume explores perception, constraint, silence, stewardship, and the role of the maker as custodian rather than originator.
      </p>


