      <h2>Suggested Citation</h2>

      <blockquote>
        Deters, J. (2026). <em>Latent Form Theory</em> (Vols. I–III). Retrieved from https://www.latentformtheory.com
      </blockquote>


      <h2>Classroom Use</h2>

      <p>
        Classroom and educational use of this material is welcomed. Instructors are encouraged to assign, excerpt, or discuss these volumes in coursework on design, craft, material culture, or aesthetics.
      </p>


      <h2>Critique & Discussion</h2>

      <p>
        Like any philosophical framework, Latent Form Theory is expected to evolve through criticism, application, and refinement. Thoughtful disagreement, counterexamples, and alternative interpretations are welcomed as part of evaluating its explanatory value.
      </p>


      <h2>Related Thinkers and Writers</h2>

      <p>
  The following thinkers address questions of craft, perception, material,
  judgment, and form that intersect with Latent Form Theory.
</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Aristotle</li>
        <li>John Ruskin</li>
        <li>William Morris</li>
        <li>Michael Polanyi</li>
        <li>Juhani Pallasmaa</li>
        <li>Richard Sennett</li>
        <li>David Pye</li>
        <li>Jun’ichirō Tanizaki</li>
        <li>George Kubler</li>
      </ul>


      
